AB  - Pre-signing contract review in procurement and R&D projects is critical for manufacturing enterprises, yet the process remains predominantly manual. Stringent data sovereignty constraints often preclude the use of cloud-based Large Language Models (LLMs), necessitating privacy-preserving on-premises solutions. This paper presents a multi-agent orchestrated local LLM framework for automated clause analysis and multi-dimensional risk assessment. The system employs mid-scale off-the-shelf LLMs grounded in an Enterprise Contract Knowledge Base (ECKB) containing policies, standard templates, and historical cases. A central Controller decomposes contracts into dimension-specific subtasks handled by specialized Risk Agents (e.g., Legal, Commercial, Technical), which utilize Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to access relevant policy snippets. A Fusion Agent then consolidates these outputs into a unified risk profile. Experiments on realistic synthetic manufacturing contracts demonstrate that the proposed Multi-Agent RAG (MA-RAG) framework significantly outperforms single-agent baselines in identifying high-risk clauses. The results validate that decomposing complex review tasks into specialized agent roles, combined with knowledge grounding, is a highly effective strategy for achieving robust contract assessment under strict privacy and resource constraints.
