TI  - Addressing Documentation Issues in Construction Claims Using Natural Language Processing
AU  - Ranjan, Rajiv
AU  - Jagannathan, Murali
AU  - Chandran, P. Ravi
AU  - Doloi, Hemanta
T2  - Journal of Legal Affairs and Dispute Resolution in Engineering and Construction
AB  - Claims are an integral part of the construction industry, vital in maintaining project timelines, cash flow, and stakeholder relationships. However, inefficient claim correspondence has become a significant challenge, often hindered by repetitive clarification cycles, inconsistent documentation, and reliance on manual processes. These issues collectively lead to delays and inefficiencies in the claim finalization process. Emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence (AI) and natural language processing (NLP), can provide promising solutions to address these challenges while ensuring data privacy. This study presents the timeline of claim correspondence and identifies 26 factors that influenced the timeline. A Python-based NLP model that utilizes spaCy’s en_core_web_lg and en_core_web_sm models is fine-tuned for named entity recognition (NER). The en_core_web_lg model demonstrated an overall accuracy of 79%, while the en_core_web_sm model recorded an accuracy of 76%. The conceptual model identifies critical elements of a claim letter cause, effect, entitlement, and substantiation, ensuring completeness and alignment with organizational standards. By leveraging historical claim data from successful claims to create a reference database, the conceptual model provides a structured framework for evaluating the completeness of newly drafted claims. Furthermore, spaCy’s displaCy visualization tool enables users to review extracted key information interactively, simplifying and enhancing the evaluation processes. This approach shows the potential to streamline claim preparation and lays the foundation for future automation in claim correspondence within the construction industry. DOI: 10.1061/JLADAH.LADR-1348. © 2025 American Society of Civil Engineers.
