TI  - Bridging the research–practice gap in construction contract management with NLP and LLMs
AU  - Tang, Yinqiu
AU  - Zheng, Chunmo
AU  - Kassem, Mohamad
AU  - Xie, Xiang
AU  - Su, Xing
T2  - Automation in Construction
AB  - Construction contracts are inherently complex, presenting significant challenges to effective and efficient contract management. Despite rapid advances in Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Large Language Models (LLMs), their adoption in construction contract management remains limited and fragmented. To date, the extent to which existing NLP and LLMs research addresses practical industry needs remains unclear, as do the key gaps and pathways required to close them. This paper systematically examines both academic research on NLP and LLMs and the practical needs of the construction industry. The findings highlight (1) a disconnect between academic research and industry needs, with existing work providing only partial coverage of the identified industry requirements; (2) that traditional NLP methods are unlikely to fulfill these requirements; (3) that LLM-based approaches show promise in addressing contract management tasks where traditional NLP falls short, though several challenges remain. To guide future progress, the paper proposes a research framework with key directions and practical insights.
